.TH CRYPT 3
.SH NAME
crypt, setkey, encrypt \- DES encryption
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.B char *crypt(const char *key, const char *salt);
.PP
.B void setkey(const char *key);
.PP
.B void encrypt(char *block, int edflag);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Crypt
is the password encryption routine.
It is based on the NBS Data Encryption Standard, with
variations intended (among other things) to frustrate use of hardware
implementations of the DES for key search.
.PP
The first argument to
.I crypt
is a user's typed password.
The second is a 2-character string chosen from the
set [a-zA-Z0-9./].
The
.I salt
string is used to perturb the DES algorithm in one of 4096
different ways, after which the password
is used as the key to encrypt repeatedly a constant string.
The returned value points to the encrypted password,
in the same alphabet as the salt.
The first two characters are the salt itself.
.PP
The other entries provide (rather primitive)
access to the actual DES algorithm.
The argument of
.I setkey
is a character array of length 64 containing only the characters
with numerical value 0 and 1.
If this string is divided into groups of 8,
the low-order bit in each group is ignored,
leading to a 56-bit key which is set into the machine.
.PP
The argument to the
.I encrypt
entry is likewise a character array of length 64
containing 0's and 1's.
The argument array is modified in place
to a similar array
representing the bits of the argument after having been
subjected to the DES algorithm using the key set by
.I setkey.
If
.I edflag
is 0, the argument is encrypted;
if non-zero,
it is decrypted.
.PP
.B Note:
none of the three is declared by any header.  v7 had none and C11 has no such
routines, so a caller declares them itself \- which is the tree's rule for a
routine no v7 header names.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
passwd(1), passwd(5), login(1), getpass(3), getpwent(3)
.SH BUGS
The return value
points to static data whose content is overwritten
by each call.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS One array, where v7 had two
This is the one change that had to be made for the
.I machine
rather than for the language, and it is the sharpest example in the library of
what word addressing does to a PDP-11 assumption.
.PP
v7 writes
.B "static char L[32], R[32];"
and then
.B indexes past the end of the first:
the initial permutation runs
.B L[j]
out to j == 63, which worked because the PDP-11 laid the two arrays adjacent with
nothing between.
.PP
.B Six chars pack into a word here,
so a 32-character array occupies six words with four bytes to spare, and
.B L[32]
is that padding \- not
.BR R[0] .
The two are one array of 64 now, with
.B L
and
.B R
as macros naming its halves.
.PP
.B The symptom, if this is ever undone, is not a crash.
.I Crypt
goes on producing thirteen plausible characters, and they are the wrong ones.
That is why lib/test/pwent pins six vectors taken from the
.I host's
crypt(3) and not from this library's own first output.
.SS The tables are cheaper than they look
Six chars to a word again: the S boxes are 512 chars, which is 86 words and not
512 of them, and the key schedule is 128 words.
.PP
The implementation is v7's bit-at-a-time DES, ported as it stands \- every array
holds one bit per char, which is why the permutation tables read exactly as the
standard prints them, and why it is as slow as it is.  None of that is worth
improving: the point is to produce the same thirteen characters v7 produced, so
that an /etc/passwd written by one is readable by the other.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/crypt.c
